🌐 Types of Computer Networks


📌 Introduction

A computer network is a group of two or more independent computers and devices connected to share data, resources, and services. These connections can be established using wired media or wireless technologies.

For example, sending a message from a smartphone to a laptop or accessing data from a remote server is possible because of computer networks.

🔹 Classification of Computer Networks

Computer networks can be classified based on:

Geographical Area

Transmission Medium

Ownership and Access Control

📍 Classification Based on Geographical Area

This classification is based on the physical distance or area covered by the network.

1️⃣ Personal Area Network (PAN)

A PAN connects personal devices within a very short range around a single user.

Coverage Range: 1–10 meters

Key Technologies: Bluetooth, NFC, Infrared

Example: Smartphone connected to wireless earbuds

Usage: Short-range personal device communication

2️⃣ Local Area Network (LAN)

A LAN connects devices within a limited area such as a home, office, or building.

Coverage Range: Room, building, or small campus

Key Technologies: Ethernet, Wi-Fi

Example: Home or office Wi-Fi network

Usage: Fast and secure local communication

3️⃣ Campus Area Network (CAN)

A CAN connects multiple LANs within a campus or nearby buildings.

Coverage Range: University or corporate campus

Key Technologies: Ethernet, Fiber optics

Example: College or corporate campus network

Usage: Interconnecting LANs under one organization

4️⃣ Metropolitan Area Network (MAN)

A MAN connects networks across a city or metropolitan region.

Coverage Range: City or large town

Key Technologies: Fiber optics, Microwave, Metro Ethernet

Example: City-wide ISP network

Usage: High-speed city-level connectivity

5️⃣ Wide Area Network (WAN)

A WAN connects networks over very large geographical areas.

Coverage Range: Country, continent, or global

Key Technologies: Leased lines, Satellite links, Internet

Example: The Internet

Usage: Long-distance and global communication

📡 Classification Based on Transmission Medium

This classification depends on how data is transmitted between devices.

1️⃣ Broadcast Network

Uses a shared communication medium

Data sent by one node is received by all nodes

Only the destination device processes the data

Follows one-to-many communication

Mostly used in LANs

Simple design but limited scalability

Examples:

Ethernet (bus-based)

Wireless LAN (Wi-Fi)

2️⃣ Point-to-Point Network

Uses dedicated links between devices

Data travels from sender to receiver directly

Follows one-to-one communication

Data may pass through intermediate devices

Requires routing algorithms

More secure and scalable

Examples:

Internet

Leased line networks

Telephone networks

🔐 Classification Based on Ownership and Access Control

This classification focuses on who owns the network and who can access it.

1️⃣ Private Network

Owned and managed by an organization or individual

Access restricted to authorized users

Uses authentication (passwords, VPNs, tokens)

High security and control

Used for internal communication

Examples:

Corporate intranet

Office LAN

Private cloud networks

2️⃣ Public Network

Owned by service providers or government

Accessible to the general public

Minimal authentication required

Wide accessibility but lower security

Examples:

Internet

Public Wi-Fi

Mobile cellular networks

3️⃣ Hybrid Network

Combination of private and public networks

Internal parts are restricted, external access is allowed

Balances security and accessibility

Examples:

Corporate network connected to the Internet

Enterprise cloud systems

Online banking platforms

🌍 Internetwork

An Internetwork is a collection of two or more independent networks connected to function as a single logical network.

Enables communication between different networks

Uses networking devices and standard protocols

The Internet is the largest example

Key Characteristics

Consists of multiple networks (LAN, MAN, WAN)

Supports different technologies (wired/wireless)

Communication enabled using standard protocols

There are two main types: Intranet and Extranet

🏢 Intranet

An Intranet is a private network used within an organization.

Features

Access restricted to authorized users

Owned and controlled by one organization

Uses Internet protocols (TCP/IP, HTTP, FTP)

Uses

Employee portals

Internal communication

Sharing policies and documents

Internal applications (HR, payroll)

Examples

Corporate internal websites

University campus portals

Government internal systems

🌐 Extranet

An Extranet is an extension of an intranet that provides limited access to external users.

Features

Partially private network

Access for partners, vendors, customers

Requires authentication and authorization

Often implemented using VPN or secure access

Uses

B2B communication

Supply chain management

Customer and vendor portals

Examples

Supplier management systems

Online banking portals

Vendor access platforms
